        "subtitle": "Vivienne Walt reports on how the Sahara Desert could offer a truly green solution to Europe's energy problems.",
        "text": "A  For years, many Europeans have regarded the Sahara as an unknown land of little economic value or importance. But this idea may soon change completely. Politicians and scientists on both sides of the Mediterranean are beginning to focus on the Sahara's potential to provide power for Europe in the future. They believe that the desert's true value comes from the fact that it is dry and empty. Some areas of the Sahara reach 45 degrees centigrade on many afternoons. It is, in other words, a gigantic natural storehouse of solar energy.\n\nB  A few years ago, scientists began to calculate just how much energy the Sahara holds, and the answer astonished them. In theory, a 90,600-square-kilometre piece of the Sahara, smaller than Portugal and a little over 1% of its total area, could produce as much electricity as all the world's power plants put together. A smaller square of 15,500 square kilometres, about the size of Connecticut, could provide electricity for Europe's 500 million people. 'I admit I was sceptical until I did the calculations myself,' says Michael Pawlyn, director of Exploration Architecture, one of three British environmental companies that make up the Sahara Forest Project, which is testing solar plants in Oman and the United Arab Emirates. Pawlyn calls the Sahara's potential 'staggering'.\n\nC  At the moment, no one is proposing a solar power station the size of a small country. But there is a fairly well-developed technology which, its supporters say, could turn the Sahara's heat and sunlight into a major source of electricity: Concentrating Solar Power (CSP). Unlike solar panels, which turn sunlight directly into electricity, CSP uses mirrors to focus light on water pipes or boilers, producing very hot steam to drive the turbines of generators. Small CSP plants have produced power in California's Mojave Desert since the 1980s. The Sahara Forest Project proposes building CSP plants in areas below sea level (the Sahara has several of these depressions), so that sea water can flow into them. This water would then be purified and used to power turbines and to wash dust off the mirrors. The waste water would then irrigate the areas around the stations, creating green oases: hence the 'forest' in the group's name.\n\nD  But producing large quantities of electricity means building huge arrays of mirrors and pipes across hundreds of miles of remote desert, which is expensive. Gerry Wolff, an engineer who heads DESERTEC, an international group of solar-power scientists, says they have estimated that it will cost about $59 billion to begin transmitting power from the Sahara by 2020.\n\nE  Building plants is only part of the challenge. One of the drawbacks of CSP technology is that it works at maximum efficiency only in sunny, hot climates, and deserts tend to be far from population centres. To supply 20% of Europe's electricity needs, more than 19,300 kilometres of cables would have to be laid under the Mediterranean, says Gunnar Asplund, head of HVDC research at ABB Power Technologies in Ludvika, Sweden. Indeed, to use renewable sources of power, including solar, wind and tidal power, Europe will need to build completely new electricity grids. This is because the existing infrastructure, built mainly for the coal-fired plants that supply 80% of Europe's power, would not be suitable for carrying the amount of electricity generated by the Sahara. Germany's government-run Aerospace Centre, which carries out energy research, estimates that replacing those lines could raise the cost of building solar plants in the Sahara and sending significant amounts of power to Europe to about $465 billion over the next 40 years. Generous government subsidies will be needed. 'Of course it costs a lot of money,' says Asplund. 'It's a lot cheaper to burn coal than to make solar power in the Sahara.'\n\nF  Meanwhile, some companies are getting started. Abengoa, an engineering company from Seville, is building one solar thermal plant in Algeria and another in Morocco, while a third is being built in Egypt by a Spanish-Japanese joint venture. The next step will be to get cables in place. Although the European Parliament has passed a law that helps investors who support the continent's goal of getting 20% of its power from renewable energy by 2020, it could take years to create the necessary infrastructure.\n\nG  Nicholas Dunlop, secretary-general of e-Parliament, a London-based NGO, thinks companies should begin transmitting small amounts of solar power as soon as the North African plants start operating, by laying a few cable lines under the Mediterranean. 'I call it the Lego method,' he says. 'Build it piece by piece. If it can be shown that power from the Sahara can be produced profitably, companies and governments will soon jump in. If they do, perhaps airline passengers flying across the Sahara will one day count the mirrors and patches of green instead of staring at sand.'"
